const faqs = [
  ["What is a clinical trial Plain Language Summary?","A clinical trial Plain Language Summary, often abbreviated as PLS, communicates information about a clinical study in language intended to be understandable to people without specialist medical or clinical research knowledge. Depending on the applicable framework and purpose, it may explain why the study was conducted, who participated, what treatments or interventions were studied, what researchers found, what safety information was observed, what limitations apply, and where readers can obtain additional information."],
  ["What is the difference between a Clinical Study Report and a Plain Language Summary?","A Clinical Study Report is a detailed scientific and regulatory account of how a clinical trial was designed, conducted, analyzed, and interpreted. A Plain Language Summary communicates key study information and findings for non-specialist readers. The PLS may be based on information contained in the CSR and related approved results, but it serves a different audience and communication purpose."],
  ["What is the difference between a clinical trial results summary and a layperson summary?","A scientific clinical trial results summary presents study results in a structured form appropriate to regulatory, scientific, registry, or professional use. A layperson or Plain Language Summary communicates relevant study information in language designed for the public and other non-specialist readers."],
  ["Does EU CTR require a layperson summary of clinical trial results?","Yes. Article 37(4) of Regulation (EU) No 536/2014 requires a summary of clinical trial results to be submitted within the applicable timeline and accompanied by a summary written in a manner understandable to laypersons. Annex V specifies the content to be included in the layperson summary. Language and submission requirements should be evaluated according to the Member States concerned, the individual trial, and applicable regulatory requirements."],
  ["What information is included in an EU CTR layperson summary?","Annex V identifies content including clinical trial identification, sponsor information, general information about the trial, the participant population, investigational medicinal products, adverse reactions and their frequency, overall results, comments on the outcome, information about planned follow-up trials, and information on where additional details may be found."],
  ["Can Sesen translate an already approved Plain Language Summary?","Yes. When a sponsor, CRO, or medical writing team already has an approved source PLS, Sesen can manage terminology preparation, multilingual translation, independent review, readability QA, numerical and consistency checks, formatting, and final delivery."],
  ["Can Sesen support plain-language development before translation?","Yes. When a project requires support before multilingual translation, Sesen can help adapt approved scientific results into clearer patient- or public-facing source content as part of a controlled workflow. Scientific interpretation and conclusions remain anchored to sponsor-approved source materials and appropriate clinical or scientific review."],
  ["How do you maintain scientific accuracy when simplifying trial results?","Sesen starts from the approved scientific source and uses study-specific references, terminology resources, professional clinical linguists, independent review, numerical checks, and final QA. Plain-language adaptation focuses on making communication easier to understand without adding conclusions not supported by the study."],
  ["How do you evaluate readability in translated Plain Language Summaries?","Readability review considers the target language as communication for its intended reader rather than as a literal mirror of English. Review can address sentence structure, terminology, jargon, explanations, acronyms, tone, number presentation, information hierarchy, local writing conventions, and overall clarity."],
  ["Do Plain Language Summaries need to be translated for CTIS?","EU CTR establishes the requirement for a layperson summary, but language requirements should be determined according to the Member States concerned and the applicable regulatory and trial context. Sesen can support the languages required for a specific CTIS disclosure program rather than assuming that every trial has the same multilingual requirements."],
  ["Can Sesen maintain terminology across the CSR, trial registry, results summary, and PLS?","Yes. Sesen uses glossaries, translation memory, sponsor-approved terminology, reference documents, style guidance, and version control to help maintain terminology relationships across the clinical documentation set. For patient-facing content, this means preserving the same underlying clinical concept at the appropriate level of technicality."],
  ["Can Sesen translate tables, charts, figures, and infographics?","Yes. Sesen supports translation and multilingual formatting of tables, figures, captions, chart labels, legends, footnotes, infographics, and other visual results content. Final QA can check both translated text and its relationship to numbers, graphics, treatment groups, and surrounding content."],
  ["Does Sesen use AI for clinical trial results translation?","Sesen uses AI-assisted tools where appropriate to support terminology extraction, translation memory leverage, consistency analysis, number and unit checks, completeness review, and quality validation. For high-stakes scientific and patient-facing content, AI operates within a human-reviewed workflow."],
  ["What languages does Sesen support?","Sesen supports clinical trial translation in 150+ languages across Europe, Asia-Pacific, North America, Latin America, the Middle East, and Africa. Language teams are selected according to the clinical subject matter, content type, target audience, and project requirements."],
  ["Can Sesen support multi-study or ongoing clinical disclosure programs?","Yes. Sesen can support individual studies as well as recurring multilingual results programs across sponsors, CROs, therapeutic areas, countries, and study portfolios. Translation memory, terminology assets, workflow templates, version management, and centralized project coordination help improve consistency and efficiency as programs scale."],
];

